  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe weather pattern is setting up to allow a parade of shortwave \ntroughs to push through the area from Thursday into the weekend, \nbringing scattered rain showers and thunderstorm chances into next \nweek.\n\nThe first shortwave will arrive Thursday morning as SSW winds push \nhumid air into the region. Surface heating combined with dewpoints \nin the low 70s will allow CAPE values to climb into the 1500-2000 \nj/kg range in the afternoon across much of the region. Bulk shear \nvalues look weak, but enhanced mid-level winds associated with the \naforementioned trough will allow for the development of marginally \norganized storm clusters/linear segments. Isolated wind damage will \nbe possible, especially east of the Finger Lakes where shear looks \nto be a little stronger. These storms should fizzle out by the mid-\nevening hours. To go along with the isolated wind damage threat, \nheavy rain is expected to develop as PWATs climb to the 2in range \nacross much of the area. We are not seeing much threat for training \nor very slow moving storms, but with the recent heavy rains, flash \nflood guidance is pretty low and a 30 min period of heavy rain could \ncause flash flooding issues, especially across the Southern Tier, \nNEPA and Catskills.\n\nFriday will see another shortwave move through that should kick off \na line of showers and storms with heavy rain that could lead to \nflash flooding. The severe threat looks lower for Friday as bulk \nshear is in the low 20kts across the area with CAPE in the 1000-1500 \nj/kg, but an isolated storm could become severe with damaging winds \nas the main threat.  \n\nTemperatures will be slightly above normal Thursday and Friday and \nwith the increased humidity, the heat index is expected to rise into \nthe low to mid 90s in our valleys both afternoons. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe weather pattern is not progged to change much this weekend and \ninto next week, with the Bermuda High sitting out near Bermuda, \nkeeping our winds mostly SSW while a strong ridge remains out west \nand a broad trough sits over central Canada. The positioning of \nthese features puts mostly zonal flow overhead with a cavalcade of \nweak shortwaves moving through the pattern. This keeps rain and \nthunderstorm chances active into next week, but severe chances look \nlow as the parameters are currently lacking for a severe weather \noutbreak. Isolated periods of heavy rain will be possible this \nweekend, although PWATs look to fall below 1.5in by Saturday so \nwidespread heavy rain does not look likely. But given our low FF \nGuidance numbers, a heavy downpour could cause some flooding\nissues.",
